{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pulls/21","id":5000077378,"node_id":"PR_kwDOSuRVDc8AAAABKgcgQg","html_url":"https://github.com/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pull/21","diff_url":"https://github.com/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pull/21.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pull/21.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/issues/21","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pulls/21/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pulls/21/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/issues/21/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/statuses/a4722f7e31e90b732c56ae1df104d9cccffe2b15","number":21,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Add MCPSTG-SUPP-06: Test signed and publicly logged tool-manifest revisions","user":{"login":"intelligent-ears","id":186805503,"node_id":"U_kgDOCyJs_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/186805503?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears","html_url":"https://github.com/intelligent-ears","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Gap\r\n\r\nSection 3.13 covers rug pulls well, but the tool-manifest pinning it describes ([What to snapshot](https://github.com/OWASP/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide/blob/main/03.%20MCP%20Threats%20and%20Attack%20Patterns/3.13-supply-chain.md#what-to-snapshot)) is local trust-on-first-use: each client hashes whatever `tools/list` returned the first time it connected. No test checks whether manifest revisions are signed by the expected publisher and recorded in a public, append-only transparency log.\r\n\r\nThat leaves three gaps:\r\n\r\n1. **First use is unverified.** If the server is malicious at first contact, the pin locks the malicious manifest in.\r\n2. **First use happens per machine.** Every laptop and CI runner pins separately, so a server can show the reviewer one manifest and a target another, and every local pin still passes. Only a shared log lets clients compare what they were served (split-view detection, as in Certificate Transparency).\r\n3. **A legitimate update looks the same as a rug pull.** A changed hash says something moved, not who published the new revision.\r\n\r\nThe local hash plus stop-and-ask in What to snapshot stays the baseline control (scored under SUPP-05 and TOOL-07). SUPP-06 proposes signed, logged revisions as an addition on top of it.\r\n\r\n## What changed\r\n\r\n- **New test `MCPSTG-SUPP-06`** (Medium, Hybrid), written in full to the `test-case-format.md` template. Six lab cases: an unsigned manifest at first use, a signed and logged revision (the positive control), a signed but unlogged revision, a revision from the wrong signer, a split view across two equally-authorized clients, and runtime drift with no new log entry. Three verdicts: Secure, Insecure (the client claims to verify and then accepts a bad envelope), and Not tested (no verifier, or the positive control fails). Includes a canonical-form procedure (RFC 8785 JCS + SHA-256), a synthetic envelope example (explicitly non-standard), and pseudocode for the admission check.\r\n- **New subsection** \"Local pins are trust-on-first-use\" under *Rug pulls and tool-description drift*.\r\n- **Provenance and signing:** a new mechanism-table row, plus a note that package provenance covers the package bytes but not the `tools/list` a remote server returns at runtime.\r\n- **Control matrix** row for malicious servers, rug pulls, and provenance. The drift list, test chains, and the 3.05 rug-pull `Tests:` line now reference SUPP-06.\r\n- **Appendix D:** placeholders `<TEST_USER_B>`, `<LAB_PUBLISHER_IDENTITY>`, `<LAB_OTHER_IDENTITY>`, `<LAB_TLOG>`.\r\n- **Appendix F:** a \"Transparency-log client\" tool category.\r\n- **Test count** updated from 79 to 80 in README, the chapter 3 overview, and `test-case-format.md`.\r\n- New references [30]–[33]: RFC 8785, RFC 9162, Sigstore Rekor, and SEP-3140 (closed 22 Sep 2026 without merge; may return through a working group).\r\n\r\n## Scope\r\n\r\nApplies to both local stdio and remote HTTP servers. Remote servers are the main case, because their `tools/list` can change without any package publish, so package provenance does not cover it. For local servers it matters when descriptions are generated at runtime or loaded from the network.\r\n\r\n## Severity\r\n\r\nMedium until a signing mechanism exists. The 2026-07-28 spec defines no signature, digest, or log field for tool definitions, and the MCP Registry does not record or sign them. A client without a manifest verifier is recorded as Not tested, not Insecure.\r\n\r\n## Review notes\r\n\r\n- All examples are synthetic and lab-only, using placeholders per `CONTRIBUTING.md`. The test explicitly says never to write lab entries to a public transparency log.\r\n- Mappings: OWASP MCP04:2025 (and MCP03:2025), CWE-347, CWE-494, and MITRE ATLAS AML.T0010.005. CWE-347 is used instead of CWE-345, which MITRE marks as Discouraged for mapping.\r\n- References to `MCPSTG-TOOL-07` are plain text until #19 lands. #16 and #19 also change the test count and the 3.05 `Tests:` line; I'll rebase after they merge.\r\n- SUPP-01 to SUPP-05 are still the generic template text. I've left them untouched here to keep this PR reviewable, and can follow up with separate PRs that make them concrete in the same style.\r\n\r\nAddresses the review request from @vermava.","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-24T23:22:37Z","updated_at":"2026-11-10T14:53:25Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"b6431e31f1408486c87f737ccadb005a399466ff","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"intelligent-ears:supp-06-logged-manifest-revisions","ref":"supp-06-logged-manifest-revisions","repo":{"id":1432663955,"node_id":"R_kgDOVWS3kw","name":"MCP-Security-Testing-Guide","full_name":"intelligent-ears/MCP-Security-Testing-Guide","owner":{"login":"intelligent-ears","id":186805503,"node_id":"U_kgDOCyJs_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/186805503?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears","html_url":"https://github.com/intelligent-ears","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/intelligent-ears/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type"